{"id": "aps-1.4-d3-1", "topic": "1.4", "dok": 3, "role": "focus", "skill": "4.B", "lo": ["UNC-1.C", "UNC-1.D"], "frq_pattern": "truncated-axis-which-display-is-honest-and-what-it-misleads", "dok_rationale": "Part (c) requires adjudicating between two displays of identical data by identifying the construction feature (where the vertical axis starts) that governs what bar height means, quantifying the distortion it creates, and then defending a display choice for a stated comparison; the graphs disagree visually, so the student must reason from the rule, not from appearance.", "stem": "Suppose 184 students at a school answered one survey question: ``Which lunch option is your favorite?'' The cafeteria newsletter printed \textbf{Graph A} under the headline ``\textbf{Pizza wins by a landslide.}'' A student council member redrew the same data as \textbf{Graph B} and said, ``It's basically a tie.''", "first_take": "Look at the two graphs. Is pizza clearly the favorite? One sentence, and say which graph you looked at.", "parts": [{"label": "a", "dok": 1, "skill": "2.A", "prompt": "Using either graph, how many students chose Pizza and how many chose Pasta? How many more chose Pizza than Pasta?"}, {"label": "b", "dok": 2, "skill": "2.B", "prompt": "Describe the distribution of favorite lunch option for these 184 students in context: which options were most and least common, and by how much (use counts or percents)."}, {"label": "c", "dok": 3, "skill": "4.B", "prompt": "A reader asks, ``Is pizza \emph{clearly} the favorite?'' Decide which graph the reader should trust to answer that, and cite the one design feature that settles it. Use the numbers to show what Graph A exaggerates: how many times taller does the Pizza bar \emph{look} than the Pasta bar, and how much bigger is 52 than 41, really? Then state whether a bar graph or a pie chart is the better display for comparing these four options, and why."}], "visual": "lunch_bars", "answers": {"a": "Pizza 52, Pasta 41 (the same on both graphs; the data did not change). Pizza has 11 more students than Pasta.", "b": "Pizza was the most common favorite (52 of 184, about 28\%), followed by Tacos (47, about 26\%) and Salad (44, about 24\%); Pasta was least common (41, about 22\%). The four options are close together: the most and least common differ by only 11 students (about 6 percentage points), and no option reached 30\% of the students. Context (favorite lunch option, 184 students) and counts or percents present.", "c": "Graph B. The settling feature is where the vertical axis starts: Graph B's axis starts at 0, so each bar's height is proportional to its count (EK UNC-1.C.2); Graph A's axis starts at 40, so each bar shows only the part of its count above 40 (12, 7, 4, 1). In Graph A the Pizza bar looks about 12 times as tall as the Pasta bar (12 units vs.\ 1 unit above the 40 line), but 52 is only 11 more than 41, about 27\% more (52/41 = 1.27), and only 28\% vs.\ 22\% of the whole school. So pizza is the favorite by a small margin, not clearly or by a landslide. Display: a bar graph is better for comparing these four options, because bar heights (from 0) can be compared directly, while pie wedges of 28\%, 26\%, 24\%, and 22\% differ by a few degrees of angle and are hard to tell apart by eye; a pie chart is for showing each option's share of the one whole, which is not the question asked. Accept a pie-chart answer only if it explains that the question is about shares of the whole AND acknowledges that the close wedges are difficult to compare."}, "scoring": {"expectedElements": [{"id": "picks-graph-b-axis-zero", "description": "Chooses Graph B and names the axis origin (starts at 0 vs. 40) as the feature that settles it, tying it to bar height representing the count", "required": true}, {"id": "quantifies-distortion", "description": "Shows the exaggeration with numbers: the Pizza bar looks roughly 12 times Pasta's (or `many times') in Graph A, while 52 vs. 41 is only 11 students / about 27\% more / about 6 percentage points", "required": true}, {"id": "display-choice-with-reason", "description": "Chooses a display (bar graph expected) for comparing the four options and gives a reason about what the display makes easy or hard to compare", "required": true}], "scoringGuide": {"E": "Graph B with the axis-starts-at-zero feature explained through bar height, the distortion quantified both ways (looks ~12x vs. actually 27\% more or 11 students), and a display choice with a comparison-based reason.", "P": "Two of the three elements, OR all three present but the feature is named only as `Graph A is zoomed in' with no link to what bar height means, OR the distortion is asserted (`it looks way bigger') with no numbers on one side.", "I": "Trusts Graph A, or picks Graph B with no reference to the axis, or gives no numbers, or chooses a display with no reason."}, "commonMistakes": ["`Graph A is wrong' --- the data in Graph A are correct; it is the axis that misleads. The counts read the same from both graphs", "Says Graph B `has more information' instead of naming the axis origin", "Compares 12 to 1 as if those were the counts (reading bar height in Graph A as the count)", "Chooses the pie chart `because it shows percents' without noticing that 28\% and 22\% wedges look nearly identical", "Concludes `it's a tie' --- Pizza IS the most common; the honest claim is `most common by a small margin'"]}, "sentence_frames": ["The reader should trust \blank[0.8in] because its vertical axis starts at \blank[0.5in], so bar height is proportional to \blank[1in].", "In Graph A the Pizza bar looks about \blank[0.5in] times as tall as the Pasta bar, but 52 is only \blank[0.6in]\% more than 41."], "source": "original", "hypothetical": true}
{"id": "aps-1.4-d2-1", "topic": "1.4", "dok": 2, "role": "reinforcement", "skill": "2.B", "lo": ["UNC-1.C"], "dok_rationale": "Converting four counts to relative frequencies and explaining why the bar graph keeps its shape applies one known procedure across cases and connects it to the count-to-proportion rule.", "stem": "Redraw the survey as a \textbf{relative-frequency} bar graph: compute the percent of the 184 students who chose each option (nearest whole percent), then sketch the four bars with the vertical axis in percents starting at 0. Explain in one sentence why the bars keep the same relative heights as Graph B even though the axis changed from counts to percents.", "answers": {"a": "Pizza 52/184 = 28\%, Tacos 47/184 = 26\%, Salad 44/184 = 24\%, Pasta 41/184 = 22\% (sum 100\%). Sketch: four bars of heights 28, 26, 24, 22 from a zero baseline, equal widths, gaps between them. Every count is divided by the same total (184), so every bar is scaled by the same factor and the comparisons between bars do not change."}, "source": "original", "hypothetical": true}
